[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Resilience and Fault Tolerance in .NET with Polly"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Modern distributed systems and microservices architectures are inherently prone to transient failures, such as momentary network glitches, database timeouts, or rate limits enforced by downstream APIs. Implementing resilience and fault tolerance is critical to ensure that your .NET applications remain stable and responsive under adverse conditions."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Introduction to Polly"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Polly is a comprehensive .NET resilience and transient-fault-handling library that allows developers to express policies such as Retry, Circuit Breaker, Rate Limiting, Timeout, Bulkhead Isolation, and Fallback in a fluent and thread-safe manner."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"Prerequisites and Installation"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"To get started with Polly in a modern .NET 8 application, you need to install the core Polly package and the extensions for dependency injection and HTTP integration."},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"dotnet add package Microsoft.Extensions.Http.Polly
dotnet add package Polly"},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Configuring Retry Policies"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Retry policy automatically retries an operation if it throws a specified exception or returns a transient failure result. Exponential backoff combined with jitter is recommended to prevent overwhelming struggling services."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"var retryPolicy = Policy
    .Handle<HttpRequestException>()
    .WaitAndRetryAsync(3, retryAttempt => TimeSpan.FromSeconds(Math.Pow(2, retryAttempt)));

await retryPolicy.ExecuteAsync(async () =>
{
    // Place your transient network call here
});"},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Implementing a Circuit Breaker"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"When a downstream service is completely down, continuously retrying requests wastes resources and delays failure responses. A Circuit Breaker monitors failures and stops sending requests when a threshold is breached, giving the service time to recover."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"var circuitBreakerPolicy = Policy
    .Handle<HttpRequestException>()
    .CircuitBreakerAsync(
        exceptionsAllowedBeforeBreaking: 3,
        durationOfBreak: TimeSpan.FromSeconds(30)
    );"},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Applying Rate Limiting"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"Rate limiting protects your application and external APIs from being flooded with too many requests within a specific time window. .NET provides built-in rate limiting abstractions that work seamlessly alongside Polly."},{"order":16,"type":"code","text":"builder.Services.AddRateLimiter(options =>
{
    options.AddFixedWindowLimiter("fixed", config =>
    {
        config.PermitLimit = 10;
        config.Window = TimeSpan.FromSeconds(10);
        config.QueueLimit = 2;
    });
});"},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"Combining Policies with PolicyWrap"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"In real-world applications, you rarely use a single resilience strategy. You can combine Retry, Circuit Breaker, and Timeout into a single cohesive pipeline using PolicyWrap."},{"order":19,"type":"code","text":"var combinedPolicy = Policy.WrapAsync(retryPolicy, circuitBreakerPolicy);

await combinedPolicy.ExecuteAsync(async () =>
{
    // Executed with retry and circuit breaker protections
});"},{"order":20,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":21,"bullet_point":"Always define transient exceptions carefully to avoid retrying unrecoverable business logic errors."},{"order":22,"bullet_point":"Use exponential backoff with jitter for retries to prevent the thundering herd problem."},{"order":23,"bullet_point":"Integrate Polly policies with IHttpClientFactory for clean dependency injection and lifecycle management."}]
